* “<span class="plainlinks">[http://ed2.oed.com/cgi/display/00143950 menace, ''n.'']</span>” listed in the ''[[w:Oxford English Dictionary|Oxford English Dictionary]]'' [2nd Ed.; 1989]

===Etymology 2===
First attested in 1303: from the {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|menacer}}, {{m|fro|manecier}}, {{m|fro|manechier}} and the {{etyl|xno|en}} {{m|fro|manasser}}, from the assumed {{etyl|VL.|en}} *{{term||mināciāre}}, from the {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|minācia}}, whence [[menace#Etymology 1|the noun]].

====Verb====
{{en-verb|menaces|menacing|menaced}} {{tcx|ambitransitive|lang=en}}

# To make threats against (someone); to [[intimidate]].
#: ''to '''menace''' a country with war''
#* Shakespeare
#*: My master {{...}} did '''menace''' me with death.
# To threaten (an evil to be inflicted).
#* Shakespeare
#*: By oath he '''menaced''' / Revenge upon the cardinal.
# To [[endanger]] (someone or something); to [[imperil]] or [[jeopardize]].

====Translations====
{{trans-top|to make threats against, to intimidate}}
* Finnish: {{t+|fi|uhkailla}}, {{t+|fi|pelotella}}
{{trans-mid}}
* Maori: {{t|mi|whakahakahaka}}
{{trans-bottom}}

